    "body": "AI-generated image\nBy Alex Lennane\n19 November 2026\nExpeditors is rethinking its approach to acquisitions, and is already evaluating opportunities in technology, a major shift for a forwarder that has built most of its global network through organic growth.\n“For the last 10 years, we have been very quiet in this zone and not looking at M&A almost in any regard. That has changed,” chief financial officer Dave Hackett told the company’s first investor day in 11 years.\n“We are evaluating various things in the tech space right now,” he added, but gave no details of the opportunities under review or what form any transaction might take.\nExpeditors ranked strategic transactions second among its capital allocation priorities, behind organic investment and ahead of returning excess cash to shareholders.\nThe prospect of M&A prompted an analyst to ask how Expeditors would integrate another business while preserving the culture its executives had spent much of the morning describing.\nEach of its 173 districts runs its own profit and loss account, with district managers’ pay tied to local performance. The offices have discretion over how they serve customers and invest in their teams, within common operating standards and systems. The senior leadership team has an average tenure of 27 years. CEO Dan Wall started as a messenger, moved into air import operations, and was running the Denver district by the age of 23.\n“We don’t want to change our culture. We don’t intend to change our culture,” said Mr Hackett. He pointed to technology capabilities as an example of an area where Expeditors had historically been quiet, even for smaller transactions.\nMr Wall said any deal would have to be “something that just complements what we’re already doing and doesn’t take away from everything else we built”.\nThose assurances follow an unusual round of job cuts at a forwarder long known for retaining staff through downturns. In June, Expeditors notified Washington State of plans to cut about 230 jobs in its Global Technology division. The company later told investors it had never had a blanket no-layoff policy, and described the cuts as part of a technology modernisation.\nAt the investor day, Mr Wall said the company’s pay model helped it “avoid layoffs at the operational level” when business slowed.\nHe described the 1986 purchase of Pac Bridge, which took Expeditors into ocean freight, as probably its last major acquisition. A more recent deal shows how it has bought a specific capability: in 2020, Expeditors acquired Fleet Logistics’ digital platform to support Koho, its online less-than-truckload shipping service.\nExpeditors gave no geographic preference for deals. Kelly Blacker, president of global geographies, cited India as a market where the company was building-out domestic road freight. She also pointed to Mexico and India as places where long-established local teams could take on more business as manufacturing shifted.\nMr Hackett said Expeditors could borrow to fund an acquisition, and could cut back share buybacks to pay down debt if leverage rose above the level it wanted to carry long-term. Asked whether that meant it could be looking at a deal worth $1bn or more, he replied: “Not suggesting that.\n“We do not have a strategy to grow through M&A,” he said. “We have a strategy to monitor M&A and look for strategic positioning, strategic gaps we can fill, and generate returns.”",
